mn26:19.8 starts: ‘Enough with teaching the Dhamma
mn26:19.9 starts: That even I found hard to reach;
mn26:19.10 starts: For it will never be perceived
mn26:19.11 starts: By those who live in lust and hate.
mn26:19.12 starts: Those dyed in lust, wrapped in darkness
mn26:19.13 starts: Will never discern this abstruse Dhamma
mn26:19.14 starts: Which goes against the worldly stream,
mn26:19.15 starts: Subtle, deep, and difficult to see.’
mn26:20.10 starts: ‘In Magadha there have appeared till now
mn26:20.11 starts: Impure teachings devised by those still stained.
mn26:20.12 starts: Open the doors to the Deathless! Let them
mn26:20.13 starts: The Dhamma that the Stainless One has found.
mn26:25.6 starts: ‘I am one who has transcended all,
mn26:25.7 starts: Unsullied among all things, renouncing all,
mn26:25.8 starts: By craving’s ceasing freed. Having known this all
mn26:25.9 starts: For myself, to whom should I point as
mn26:25.10 starts: I have no teacher, and one like
mn26:25.11 starts: Exists nowhere in all the world
mn26:25.12 starts: With all its gods, because I have
mn26:25.13 starts: No person for my counterpart.
mn82:42.23 starts: While his heirs take over his wealth, this
mn82:42.24 starts: Must pass on according to his actions;
mn82:42.25 starts: And as he dies nothing can follow him;
mn82:42.26 starts: Not child nor wife nor wealth nor royal
sn1.17:3.1 starts: “Drawing in the mind’s thoughts
sn1.17:3.2 starts: As a tortoise draws its limbs into its
sn1.17:3.3 starts: Independent, not harassing others, fully quenched,
sn1.17:3.4 starts: A bhikkhu would not blame anyone.”
